<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="integrations-keycloak"
      toc={[
        {"sso", "Single sign-on"},
        {"scim", "Directory sync"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="integrations-keycloak">
        <:dek>
          Connect Keycloak to emisar: a confidential OIDC client with PKCE for sign-in. Keycloak has
          no outbound SCIM, and the directory-sync section says what that leaves you to do.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create one confidential Keycloak client, then connect it to emisar. You need Keycloak
        realm-admin access and an emisar owner or admin. Keep
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</strong>
        open in emisar so you can copy its callback URL.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Verified end to end">
        <p>
          emisar's OIDC authorization-code and just-in-time provisioning flow is verified end to
          end against Keycloak 26.7 — the screenshots below are that setup.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso">Single sign-on</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start an OpenID Connect client.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Keycloak Admin Console, choose your realm, then go to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Clients → Create client</strong>. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client type</strong>
              on <span class="text-zinc-300">OpenID Connect</span>, set
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-portal</code>,
              and click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-general.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · General settings"
              caption="Create an OpenID Connect client for the emisar web application."
              alt="Keycloak's Create client General settings step with OpenID Connect selected, emisar-portal as the client ID, and emisar as the name."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Enable only the flow emisar uses.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Turn <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client authentication</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require PKCE</strong>
              on. Keep <strong class="text-zinc-200">Standard flow</strong>
              on and leave the PKCE method on <span class="text-zinc-300">S256</span>. Leave
              Authorization, DPoP, Direct access grants, Implicit flow, Service accounts roles,
              and every device flow off. Then click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-capabilities.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Capability config"
              caption="A confidential client using the authorization-code flow with PKCE."
              alt="Keycloak's Capability config step with Client authentication, Standard flow, and Require PKCE enabled; all other capabilities and authentication flows are disabled."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Register the exact redirect URI.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Copy the callback shown in emisar and paste it into <strong class="text-zinc-200">Valid redirect URIs</strong>. It ends in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>.
              Do not add a wildcard. The other URL fields can stay empty; click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Save</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-redirect.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Login settings"
              caption="The screenshot uses a reserved example host; paste the callback from your emisar form verbatim."
              alt="Keycloak's Login settings step with https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback entered as the sole Valid redirect URI and no wildcard."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Copy the client secret.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open the saved client's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Credentials</strong>
              tab. Copy its client secret into emisar's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>
              field. Treat the value like a password; do not put it in tickets, chat, or
              screenshots.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-secret.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Credentials"
              caption="The client secret stays masked in this guide."
              alt="The Keycloak Credentials tab for emisar-portal with Client Id and Secret selected and the client secret hidden behind password dots."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Verify the identity scopes.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client scopes</strong>
              tab, confirm that <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">email</code>
              and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">profile</code>
              are assigned as <span class="text-zinc-300">Default</span>.
              emisar requests <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">openid</code>
              in the authorization request, so it does not appear as an assigned client-scope row.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-scopes.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Client scopes"
              caption="The default email and profile scopes give emisar the verified identity claims it needs."
              alt="The Keycloak Client scopes table for emisar-portal with email and profile assigned as Default scopes."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Connect and test the first sign-in.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Back in emisar, select the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Keycloak</strong>
              preset and enter the issuer URL <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
                https://YOUR-KEYCLOAK-HOST/realms/YOUR-REALM
              </code>,
              client ID, and client secret. Choose
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Auto-provision</strong>
              or <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manual</strong>
              new-user provisioning and a default role, test the connection, enable it, and save.
              In a private browser window, sign in as a Keycloak user who is not yet in emisar.
              Auto-provision should create the user at the default role; Manual should create a
              pending request for an admin instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="scim">Directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keycloak cannot drive directory sync. Its built-in SCIM support runs the other way — the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM Realm API</strong>
        added in 26.6 lets something else provision users <em>into</em>
        Keycloak — and an outbound client that pushes users to an application like emisar is on the
        roadmap, not in a release. So Keycloak gives you
        single sign-on (above)
        with members created on first sign-in, and nothing else: no group → role mapping, and nobody
        removed from emisar when they leave Keycloak. Disabling the Keycloak user stops them signing
        in, which is the control that matters; remove the member in
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team</strong>
        to close out the rest.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The connection still offers directory sync, because emisar's endpoint is ordinary SCIM 2.0
        with a bearer token and a third-party Keycloak provisioning plugin can drive it. If you run
        one, enable sync here and point it at the base URL and token above. That plugin is yours to
        choose, configure, and support — we don't ship or certify one.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
